So far, every Java program we've written has interacted with the user through the console.
A GUI replaces the console with windows, buttons, text fields, labels, and other visual components that users can interact with using a mouse and keyboard.
This topic is very common in introductory Java courses because it introduces event-driven programming, a different way of thinking compared to console applications.

A Graphical User Interface (GUI) is a visual interface that allows users to interact with a program through graphical components such as windows, buttons, menus, labels, and text fields instead of text-based commands.

Console vs GUI
A console application communicates using text.
Example: Enter your age:
         20
A GUI application communicates visually.
+-----------------------------+
| Name: [______________]      |
|                             |
| [ Submit ]                  |
+-----------------------------+
Instead of typing commands, the user clicks buttons and fills in fields.

Java GUI Libraries
Java provides two major GUI libraries.
1. AWT (Abstract Window Toolkit)
Package: java.awt
It was Java's first GUI library.
Examples: Frame
          Button
          TextField
          Label
2. Swing
Package: javax.swing
Swing is built on top of AWT and provides more powerful, flexible, and platform-independent components.
Examples: JFrame
          JButton
          JLabel
          JTextField
          JTextArea
          JPanel
For modern introductory Java programming, Swing is generally preferred.

AWT vs Swing
| AWT                           | Swing                                  |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Older library                 | Newer library                          |
| Platform-dependent components | Mostly platform-independent components |
| Fewer components              | More components                        |
| Uses `Frame`                  | Uses `JFrame`                          |
For your exams, remember: Swing extends AWT.
This means Swing builds upon AWT rather than replacing it completely.

What is a JFrame?
A JFrame is the main window of a Swing application.
Everything you see in a Swing application is usually placed inside a JFrame.
Think of it as the application's container.

Creating a JFrame
import javax.swing.JFrame;

public class MyWindow {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        JFrame frame = new JFrame();

        frame.setVisible(true);

    }

}

Output: A blank window appears.

Why Doesn't the Program Close?
If you click the X, the window disappears, but the program may continue running.
Why? Because you've only hidden the window.
The Java program itself is still executing.
To tell Java to terminate the application: frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
Now closing the window also ends the program

Common JFrame Methods
1. Set the title
frame.setTitle("Student Management");

2. Set the window size
frame.setSize(500, 400);
Width: 500 pixels
Height: 400 pixels

3. Make it visible
frame.setVisible(true);
Without this line: No window appears.

4. Close correctly
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

5. Center the window
frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);
This places the window in the center of the screen.

Putting It Together
import javax.swing.JFrame;

public class FirstGUI {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        JFrame frame = new JFrame();

        frame.setTitle("My First GUI");

        frame.setSize(400, 300);

        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);

        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        frame.setVisible(true);

    }

}

What is Event-Driven Programming?
Console programs usually execute from top to bottom.
Example: Read input
         ↓
         Process input
         ↓
         Print result
         ↓
         End
A GUI program is different. It waits for the user to do something.
Examples: Click a button.
          Type in a text field.
          Close a window.
These actions are called events. The program responds only when an event occurs.

Containers and Components
In Swing: A container holds other components.
Examples: JFrame
          JPanel
A component is something the user interacts with.
Examples: JButton
          JLabel
          JTextField
          JTextArea
Visualization: JFrame
               │
               └── JPanel
                     │
                     ├── JLabel
                     ├── JTextField
                     └── JButton
Notice: Everything ultimately sits inside the JFrame.

Example Code
import javax.swing.JFrame;

public class JFrameExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        JFrame frame = new JFrame();

        frame.setTitle("Welcome Aba");

        frame.setSize(500, 300);

        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);

        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        frame.setVisible(true);

    }

}

